This means the UNCHANGED `apiClient.guestHeaders()` (bare, no `base:` override)
branch -- the "normal, module already set" path this ticket's diff did NOT touch --
is ALSO currently sending a request the backend 400s on, in this live environment,
across every reachable trigger of `_clearCartOnline()` (the cross-module search
guard is the ONLY live UI path to it now that NEARS-1290 removed the same-store
reset dialog and left `ItemController.clearAndAddToCart` as dead code with zero
call sites). Root mechanism not fully isolated (candidates: a later
`apiClient.updateHeader()` call, e.g. from the async guest-login/zone-refresh
sequence, overwriting `_mainHeaders` without a moduleId once more after
`setModule()`'s own synchronous update set it correctly) -- flagged for the
engineer to instrument/trace `_mainHeaders['moduleId']` at the exact moment
`_clearCartOnline()`'s DELETE fires, rather than trusting the unit test's mocked
`apiClient.guestHeaders()` stub, which never exercises the real `_mainHeaders`
staleness because it's stubbed to return a fixed sentinel unconditionally.

Whether this AC4 finding is IN-SCOPE for this ticket's own diff (task_bug) or a
pre-existing, ticket-external regression (regression_bug) is genuinely ambiguous
from a QA seat: the branch itself is unchanged code, but the ticket's own AC4 text
explicitly asks QA to demonstrate it "still works" as a regression gate for this
diff, and the automated backstop's AC4 test claims to cover exactly this and is
green -- a real gap between the unit test's mock and live behavour. Reported as a
task_bug per the ticket's own AC4 framing (QA is instructed to treat this specific
regression check as this ticket's business), with a note that the underlying
`_mainHeaders` staleness mechanism may pre-date this diff and could also be
filed/cross-referenced as a broader header-staleness defect if the engineer's
trace confirms it is unrelated to anything NEARS-2783 touched.
